Description: KiwiSDR cfea79e6 — better logging when bots send bad API (partial port)
 Ported from upstream commit "better logging when bots send bad API"
 (cfea79e6bb1d129028d07b77d4b532594fdd8d58). Includes the self-contained
 C++ pieces:
  - ctprintf() helper added to support/printf.{cpp,h}: like clprintf() but
    takes an explicit printf_type so callers can route output to the log
    file (used by the upstream ext.cpp / rx_sound_cmd.cpp call sites);
  - u1_t snd_cmd_recv / wf_cmd_recv fields added to conn_t (rx/conn.h) to
    remember the last SET_INIT parameters received, so a connection can be
    logged with what it actually sent before being kicked;
  - the "incomplete connection kicked" message in support/stats.cpp now
    includes the (snd=%02x wf=%02x) parameters to help diagnose bot traffic.
 .
 NOT included from the upstream commit (context diverges / out of scope):
  - extensions/ext.cpp, rx/rx_server.cpp, rx/rx_sound.cpp and
    rx/rx_sound_cmd.cpp call-site refactors (conn_t / connection-close
    flow and firmware-selection switch differ on Web-888);
  - the removal of u4_t wf_loop, wf_lock, wf_get from conn_t (unrelated to
    the logging improvement and still used by Web-888 code).
 ctprintf() and the new conn_t fields are therefore currently unused by
 in-tree callers; they land now so later call-site ports can rely on them.
Origin: https://github.com/kiwisdr/kiwiSDR-wb66v4/ commit cfea79e6
Forwarded: not-needed

diff --git a/support/printf.cpp b/support/printf.cpp
--- a/support/printf.cpp
+++ b/support/printf.cpp
@@ -422,6 +422,16 @@
     va_end(ap);
 }
 
+// KiwiSDR port (cfea79e6 "better logging when bots send bad API"): like clprintf
+// but takes an explicit printf_type so callers can route to the log file.
+void ctprintf(conn_t* c, u4_t printf_type, const char* fmt, ...) {
+    va_list ap;
+    lock_holder holder(print_lock);
+    va_start(ap, fmt);
+    ll_printf(printf_type, c, fmt, ap);
+    va_end(ap);
+}
+
 void clfprintf(conn_t* c, u4_t printf_type, const char* fmt, ...) {
     va_list ap;
     lock_holder holder(print_lock);

